  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1154 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nBy the start of the extended forecast package, the weak shortwave \nridge will begin to translate eastward in response to a long wave \ntrough digging as far south as SOCAL begins to eject towards the \nIntermountain West. As a result of this, southwesterly flow aloft \nwill return by late Tuesday while at the surface moist upslope \nreturn flow will continue as the surface low ahead of the parent \ntrough extends into the southern Rockies while it slowly tracks \nnortheast into the High Plains. Increased moisture through the H3 to \nH7 levels in combinations to the surface levels will likely result \nin the return of stratus across the region through mid-week. \nCompared to recent days, there is indication that these clouds may \nattempt to break up and clear, mainly across the Caprock with drier \nsoutherly winds possible by the late afternoon and evening. Though \nits a bit too early for a clear and confident answer. As a result, \nWednesday high temperatures may vary depending on clearing. Current \nforecast portrays slightly warmer highs in the upper 50s to low 60s \nacross the FA. Although the official forecast remains dry, a few \nensemble members are highlighting a few perturbations tracking \nthrough the region Wednesday afternoon, which combined with \nincreased moisture present, there is a chance for an isolated shower \nor two to develop along with periods of drizzle. As of now, will \nmaintain a dry forecast, considering run to run differences. Expect \na similar synoptic pattern to continue through the work week a s a \nseries of waves translating nearby, with a rinse and repeat weather \nforecast with highs in the 50s and 60s, light southerly winds, and \nlingering stratus through the early afternoon with potential \nclearing through the afternoon. Additionally, we can expect isolated \nshowers and/or drizzle through parts of the day, similar to what is \nexpected Wednesday. Through the weekend, a shortwave will transverse \nthrough the Central Plains, with the base of the trough digging into \nportions of the Texas Panhandle. At the moment, H5 flow looks to \nturn more westerly with the bulk of moisture associated with the \nsystem placed to our east. Which will likely limit overall \nprecipitation chances this weekend. However, ensemble guidance still \nhighlights a few across across the Rolling Plains may see an \nisolated shower or two. For now, will maintain NBM non-mentionable \nPoPs until we see better agreement amongst global NWP guidance in \nthe coming days.",
